          },
          "questions_only": {
            "default": false,
            "type": "boolean",
          },
          "sort": {
            "enum": [
              "new",
              "old",
              "top",
              "active",
              "tag_relevance",
              "curated_date",
              "af_top",
            ],
            "type": "string",
          },
          "tag": {
            "maxLength": 500,
            "minLength": 1,
            "type": "string",
          },
          "user": {
            "maxLength": 500,
            "minLength": 1,
            "type": "string",
          },
        },
        "type": "object",
      },
      "name": "list_posts",
      "title": "What did X post about T in a period, best first?",
    },
    {
      "annotations": {
        "destructiveHint": false,
        "idempotentHint": true,
        "openWorldHint": true,
        "readOnlyHint": true,
      },
      "description": "List comments across the site or by one person: what someone has been commenting on, the latest comments site-wide, or quick takes. limit counts comments. Filters: user, date_from/date_to (posting date), af_only, quick_takes_only (top-level quick takes, i.e. shortform posts, only), min_karma. sort: new (default), old, top (karma). Without user, sort top and quick_takes_only each need both date_from and date_to, at most 31 days apart (e.g. date_from "2024-03-01", date_to "2024-03-31"). Dates are UTC; date_to includes the whole day, month or year it names. Quick takes, and a person's comments by top, are ranked over at most 2,000 matching comments (narrow the dates beyond that). Comments whose post hides their karma are left out of sort top and min_karma. Each comment shows where it is (its post with post_id and AF status, its wiki page, or "quick take" ("in a quick take" for a reply)), who it replies to (name and ids; no text), date, karma and votes, agreement, reactions, flags, AF karma or "LW only", its ids and its text (cut at about 1,500 tokens; get_comments comment=<comment_id> shows it whole in its thread). Not listed: comment placeholders and the turns of old-style debates. For one post's discussion use get_comments. Paging: new and old go back without limit (site-wide lists filtered by nothing but af_only stop after 500 comments). For sort top, this server reads up to about the 2,000th result in this order, and rankings can shift between pages as votes arrive.

