      },
      "description": "A tag or wiki page: its text and its top posts in one call. tag = id, URL (/w/…, /tag/…, /p/…, arbital.com/p/…) or exact slug; earlier slugs, Arbital ids and lens slugs work; a lens slug opens that lens (an extra tab of the page), and a summary_id opens the page or lens it summarizes. limit counts posts. Returns name, ids, page type (tag, core tag, wiki-only page, which the site doesn't offer as a tag, though it can hold posts, or placeholder page, not yet created), parent tag and subtags, likes on the page itself, the wiki text as Markdown (an outline and section=<number> for long pages; match=<text> keeps only the paragraphs containing it as whole words), the page's lenses (listed with lens_slug; open one by passing it as tag), short summaries (with summary_id), related Arbital pages for imported pages, the number of discussion comments (read them with get_comments tag=…), for one of the 7 core tags a preview of the tags grouped under it (list_tags gives them all), and limit tagged posts in sort order: top (karma, default), tag_relevance (strength of tagging), new, old. Which order the site's own tag page uses is not known. Only posts actually tagged are listed (relevance 1 or more); the header gives the forum's count of them. Event and meetup posts are not listed here; they open by id and can appear in search. The first page always shows the posts; a long wiki text fills the rest of the page and continues with the cursor. af_only applies to the posts (wiki pages themselves have no AF status). More posts: the list_posts call given at the end, whose cursor starts right after the last post shown.

