    {
      "annotations": {
        "destructiveHint": false,
        "idempotentHint": true,
        "openWorldHint": true,
        "readOnlyHint": true,
      },
      "description": "Who first used a phrase on LessWrong or the Alignment Forum, and was it in the original text? forms: the phrase and up to 4 other forms of it (word endings, a possessive among them, are not matched, so give "mesa optimizers" and "mesa optimizer's" yourself; hyphens count as spaces; a phrase reads across commas, not sentence or paragraph ends); a use of any form counts. Searches posts (and comments, unless include_comments=false) oldest first for each form, keeps only hits whose text really contains it, then checks the edit history of the earliest posts: was the phrase in the text as published, or added by a later edit, and when? A comment's earlier versions are not available, so a comment edited after posting holds the phrase now but can't be verified. limit counts candidates per page; the history of at most 10 candidates is checked per call, and any others on the page are listed as "not checked yet" (continue with the cursor to check them). Returns the earliest verified use, the other candidates with dates and authors (a post with it only in its title is listed, unverified), and why earlier-looking hits were ruled out (such as quoted only). The answer means "the earliest public use that could be verified", not a guaranteed first use. Search matches each item's current text only, dated by its original posting date, and leaves out posts with negative karma, unlisted posts, retracted comments and comments by authors the site never approved. So a phrase first used in one of those is missed. Texts from before 13 February 2019 exist only as a 2019 snapshot, so presence at publication can't be confirmed for them. The pre-2009 archive is nearly all Eliezer Yudkowsky's Overcoming Bias posts, 5 old essays carry placeholder 1 January dates, and a post's date can be later than its first public appearance. The forum's exact matching can't reach a word it keeps whole with punctuation inside ("Goodhart's", "Node.js", "GPT-4.5") or a word with accented letters ("Gödel"). A form holding such a word is searched loosely, oldest first, and each hit is checked for the form as written (uses of the other apostrophe form are reported separately); only the oldest hits are read, and the reply says when hits before the earliest verified use went unread. Writers often use ASCII spellings ("Godel", "Lob's theorem"), which can be given as forms. af_only searches AF items only.

